yeah house of the dragon episode 2 is  pretty freaking great i was a little  hesitant on the first episode about this  show being as good as the first four  seasons of game of thrones but episode  two really blew me away besides the best  scenes within the episode that i'll get  to later i think the direction of this  episode was masterfully done in respect  to acting cinematography pacing and  editing firstly let's talk about the  acting because holy [ __ ] the actresses  who played rainer and alison spoke  volumes with their performances in this  episode this also ties into the pacing  of the dialogue scenes but there were so  many slow moments that allowed the  camera to sit on a character's face that  let us see the subtleties of their  performance it really emphasized what  the characters were going through in  each moment and the fact that each shot  lingered on the actors and allowed them  to wow us with their performance made me  gush with the joy like the scene where  renera and allison are talking to each  other by candlelights about their mother  and father is a great example of this  there's an entire uninterrupted moment  where rainier is praying and it's just  breathtaking from her acting as she  breaks down and the music slowly feeding  in with a serene and happy tone uplifts  the seemingly normal moment to being  great and this isn't even mentioning the  gorgeous cinematography it's genius to  me that the scene was filmed next to  dozens of candle lights that eliminated  the actors faces like you see the  specular highlights within each of their  eyes as they're on the verge of crying  so even the cinematography is elevating  the already great performances and this  is just a normal chill scene that  doesn't even have major plot  significance that fundamentally raises  its quality because the plot point of  viserys having to find a new match to  marry further increased the intrigue  value of this episode this plot  development basically twisted a knife  into every character and was such an  engaging conflict because everyone had a  stake in it will cordless valerian  successfully marry off his daughter or  will other vultures swoop in and take  the prize the point of manipulation as  everyone is trying to persuade the king  into their own favor is like peak  political drama viserys being kind of a  pushover and a king that can be  manipulated makes for a perfect subject  in this regard it really shows and puts  a spotlight onto each character's  motivations and how grimy and slimy they  all are this was essentially what i love  so much about game of thrones so many of  those small council meetings that  showcase the colorful roster of  characters and their varied and  dangerous motivations really made  everyone fun to watch house of the  dragon is getting to this point with  otto hightower and cordless valerion and  there is still some time for the other  characters on the small council that  haven't been developed as much but to me  this conflict of finding a spouse is  also a perfect formula for creating  great dialogue scenes because it's  insanely layered on one hand we have the  conundrum of viserys not wanting to move  on then there's a dynamic vernier having  to accept the obligation that our father  has to remarry for legacy and then  finally we have everyone making a play  to seize the opportunity of power it's  just outstanding and it pushes every  character to a breaking point this  episode and even characters like lionel  strong got an opportunity to shine with  his own perspective but it would have  been nice if the writers were able to  write in a way to tell us the  significance of this character and a  little bit of his backstory because we  still don't really know who he is yet as  a point of comparison  cordless valerion had a great monologue  that thoroughly built him up as a  character by him diving into his  backstory but the genius thing about  this scene was that this monologue was  doubled as plot developments slight  spoiler and i'll start to mark any  spoilers with time codes just in case  anyone hasn't seen the episode but  cordless for lyrion relating himself to  daemon through his backstory and his  overarching pitch to form an alliance  with him genuinely impressed me because  it's really hard to work in a lot of  expository backstory and have it make  sense within a scene as natural dialogue  but this instance is a shining example  of it working and because of that fact  this was one of my favorite scenes in  the episode and on the subject of best  scenes we have the confrontation of  damon targaryen now this is probably a  favorite scene among everyone for this  episode and rightfully so because this  scene was essentially a master class in  suspense and tension firstly the music  in the scene perfectly aided the rising  heat and put everyone on edge although  rameen jawadi didn't make a new theme  for the title sequence which in of  itself was really sad and disappointing  he still created great music to  drastically rise attention here i even  loved the subtle editing move in which  the music for the confrontation scene  begins during otto hightower preparing  for the trip and then we cut the  dragonstone it really eases us into the  scene and it also does away with the  notion of fast traveling that was an  issue in game of thrones because here in  house of the dragon it feels like an  extension of the same scene and on that  notion the show is establishing a lot of  time jumps but the narrative still feels  very seamless so the time jumps don't  feel jarring in any way  getting back to the confrontation scene  though the cinematography and overall  design of this scene was also  intentionally orchestrated to further  punch up the suspense the thick fog  rolling over the characters and making  everything feel moody is really smart  especially since this was shot on the  volume and if anyone doesn't know what  the volume is it's a massive room with  led panels that essentially work as  real-time green screens that play  footage on the backgrounds within the  volume a small limitation is that you  can't really copy direct sunlight that  well because it's hard to match the  severe output of light from the sun so  the craters decided to set the scene in  a thick fog so this limitation of the  volume ended up actually aiding the  scene then going back to the aspect of  the pacing for a scene the shots where  we sit on ardo hightower's face and the  overall arsenal of reaction shots from  everyone made me feel even more uneasy  especially when the dragons start to  come out because man do the actors  illustrate how scared they really are  and the reveal of karaxis was so good  and i love that we first hear his squeal  before his visual introduction it really  emphasizes that oh [ __ ] this is about to  get really ugly and the shot of otto  hightower soiling himself is the cherry  on top again going into minor spoilers  the turn of the scene in which hernia  shows up was a great way to have her  gain some value in clout and prove to  people that she's really smart and brave  her dynamic with damon is really  refreshing and i like how they don't  just simply hate each other something  that i was kind of worried about in  episode one where damon might turn into  a generic villain but that worry has  completely vanished in this scene we all  see her near his new theme and i love  how they introduce it here because this  was essentially the first time she  proves herself so she's basically  earning her theme through this event  because of this that even makes her more  of a badass as she's proudly walking  through the red keep with her new theme  playing aloud and yet again this also  deletes my other worry that ramin jawadi  would use a frankenstein version of  daenerys's theme for rhonera so thank  god there's a new theme for her to  differentiate her a bit more another  aspect this episode does really well is  a political intrigue that was one major  aspect that game of thrones did  perfectly so it's really assuring to see  house of the dragon handle the political  sphere of westeros just as well all of  the small council meetings are like  crack to me because not only do these  scenes set up characters in the plot but  they also heavily develop the world in  general politics associated with the  characters the show feels very immersive  in this regard and it makes every  development that's revealed very  engaging because i just want to know  more and i love that they are putting  emphasis on the crab theater conflict  because this is something that's  impacting very specific parts of the  world like trading routes which normally  wouldn't really hear that much about but  having this detail within the world  makes it have even more depth which on  the subject of the crab feeder every  shot so far on the beach was beautiful  the shallow focus of crabs walking  around feasting on people is such a  unique site to see that it makes what is  probably a brief secondary antagonist as  being very memorable that in the design  of the crab feeder is very cool and i'm  excited to see what happens with him in  the next episode and since there will be  more action in episode 3 due to the  preview it's really nice to note that  the dialogue was able to hold up on its  own because going into the house of the  dragon i thought there would be a bit of  an over reliance on action and dragon  spectacle which thankfully that isn't  the case as evidenced by episode 2 which  virtually had no action and instead had  insane tension-inducing scenes that were  primarily driven by dialogue so the fact  that house of the dragon has proven to  have great dialogue scenes makes me  really excited to see how the action  portion of the show plays out like to me  this is the best case scenario for how  the show could have turned out in my  mind i'm genuinely at least for episode  2 struggling to find any major flaws  even the character who plays elena  valerion did a surprisingly good job  with what she had to do and perfectly  illustrated the doubt and hesitation  behind what she had to say to viserys so  for this episode i'm sitting at about a  9 from how amazing the acting  cinematography and overall writing was i  feel like i'm fully back into the world  of game of thrones and i hope the rest  of the season can retain the current  quality set by this episode thank you  for watching and let me know what you  guys thought about episode 2 of house of  the dragon
 

